     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this one, even with your confirmation. Amlodipine 50 mg daily is five times the maximum labeled dose. It has a long half-life of about 30 to 50 hours, so it would accumulate. The risk is prolonged hypotension and vasodilatory shock that can be refractory to treatment. Nothing in this patient's profile, such as normal renal and hepatic function, suggests a reason for that dose.\n\nThis isn't a judgment about your clinical authority. I just can't enter an order that looks likely to seriously harm the patient without a documented rationale.\n\nHere are some options:\n- **Enter a standard dose now.** I can enter 5 mg or 10 mg by mouth once daily. If you need more blood pressure control, you can add a second agent such as an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide.\n- **Talk to pharmacy.** If you have a specific reason for the higher dose, a pharmacist can review it with you. The order would then need to go through your institution's high-dose override process.\n\nLet me know if you'd like me to enter 5 mg or 10 mg."
      }
     ],
